<section id="human-practices-sustainability">
  <h1>Sustainability</h1>

  <p class="page-lead">
    PAGER-Yeast is an engineered-yeast sensor for influenza A. For this project, sustainability means keeping the
    system usable where influenza is most often missed — clinics without laboratory infrastructure, schools, border
    checkpoints and homes — and keeping the parts reusable by other teams. Three design decisions follow from that:
    a low-cost portable readout, a recognition module that can be re-targeted without new hardware, and an open,
    standardised parts set.
  </p>

  <h2>Designed for low-resource settings</h2>
  <p>
    The reporter is read as fluorescence intensity, which a portable fluorimeter can measure; the workflow does not
    require a qPCR thermocycler or an ELISA plate reader. The intended deployment settings are primary-care clinic
    screening, school morning checks, border quarantine ports and at-home self-testing — places where a result has to
    arrive quickly and at low cost. A semi-quantitative relationship between fluorescence intensity and viral load
    is being established through DCZ concentration-gradient and induction-time optimisation, so that a reader
    without calibration curves can still return an interpretable result.
  </p>

  <h2>A recognition module that is swapped, not rebuilt</h2>
  <p>
    The membrane anchor — the α-factor secretion signal and the linker scaffold — stays fixed, and only the VHH
    sequence is replaced to target a new antigen. Re-targeting the sensor to H5, H7, SARS-CoV-2 or RSV therefore
    reuses the same chassis, the same reporter and the same reader: extending the analyte range costs a VHH
    sequence, not a new instrument. The downstream GPCR signal rewiring and the dual-mode reporter are likewise kept
    universal across targets.
  </p>

  <h2>Open parts and reproducible protocols</h2>
  <p>
    The signalling and reporter components are being submitted as iGEM Registry-compatible parts, together with the
    protocols and plasmid maps needed to reproduce each construct. Standardised, registry-indexed parts let other
    teams reuse the module instead of re-optimising it, and keep the work auditable.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits of this assessment</h2>
  <p>
    This page states design intent and the reuse strategy; it is not a life-cycle assessment. The material footprint
    of the reader, the disposal route of the used test components and the energy demand of long-term field
    deployment have not been measured yet, and no environmental benefit is claimed on their behalf.
  </p>

